WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.290
This is Nomikos.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:05.580
It helps researchers transcribe
historical manuscripts in Greek, Syriac,

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:06.680
Coptic and Armenian.

00:00:06.985 --> 00:00:08.775
Models draft the lines and the text.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.950
You review their work
and fix what is wrong.

00:00:11.747 --> 00:00:13.417
Sign in with your email and password.

00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:15.072
Your work lives in projects.

00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:18.697
Create a new project for a manuscript
collection and give it a name.

00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:21.082
You can now pick default
models for the project,

00:00:21.232 --> 00:00:23.102
so every new job starts with them.

00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:26.503
Inside a project,
each manuscript is a document.

00:00:26.723 --> 00:00:29.313
Open it and upload the
page images, or a PDF.

00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:31.943
Each image becomes a page you can work on.

00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:34.157
The first step is segmentation.

00:00:34.362 --> 00:00:36.772
A model finds every line
of writing on the page.

00:00:37.002 --> 00:00:39.412
You can now choose which
segmentation model runs.

00:00:39.637 --> 00:00:42.667
One is called seg-1,
and the other is called seg-2.

00:00:42.807 --> 00:00:44.737
The lines appear as outlines on the image.

00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:48.963
Next, a recognition model reads
each line and writes out its text.

00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:52.263
Jobs run on your own computer
while the Nomikos agent is running,

00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:53.713
and in the cloud otherwise.

00:00:54.113 --> 00:00:55.663
Each job says which one it used.

00:00:56.513 --> 00:01:00.043
Corrections happen in the page editor,
which now splits the screen in two.

00:01:00.223 --> 00:01:01.793
The manuscript is on the left.

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:04.488
The transcript is on the right,
one row per line.

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:07.498
Click a line on the image and
its row lights up beside it.

00:01:08.147 --> 00:01:10.397
For the selected line,
you see the model output.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:13.707
Letters the model was unsure of are
coloured, so you know where to look.

00:01:13.852 --> 00:01:16.342
Accept the text as it is,
or type your correction.

00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:19.497
Sometimes the model cuts
one line into pieces.

00:01:19.772 --> 00:01:21.222
Hold shift and click each piece.

00:01:21.452 --> 00:01:23.542
Then press merge,
and they become one line.

00:01:23.747 --> 00:01:25.717
The piece with your
corrected text keeps it.

00:01:25.777 --> 00:01:29.567
You can also draw a region around a
folio or a column, and give it a label.

00:01:30.313 --> 00:01:33.663
Nomikos now keeps what the model
proposed next to what you corrected.

00:01:33.973 --> 00:01:37.143
So the model's first draft is
never lost when you edit a page.

00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:40.923
Your corrections become training data,
and the next model learns from them.

00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:43.623
When a document is ready, download it.

00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:48.918
There is a transcription PDF, plain text,
a Word file, and PAGE XML with the images.

00:01:49.213 --> 00:01:52.223
The PAGE XML now carries
real baselines and regions,

00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.123
so other tools can train on it.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.357
You can also publish a document.

00:01:56.557 --> 00:01:59.307
The project owner copies a
private link and shares it.

00:01:59.412 --> 00:02:03.222
Anyone with the link can read the
pages and the transcript side by side.

00:02:03.913 --> 00:02:05.043
Projects can be shared.

00:02:05.218 --> 00:02:07.148
Add a colleague by email or username,

00:02:07.518 --> 00:02:09.588
and they can open the project
and edit its documents.

00:02:10.023 --> 00:02:12.853
Write guidelines,
so everyone transcribes the same way.

00:02:13.547 --> 00:02:15.277
Found a problem, or have an idea?

00:02:15.472 --> 00:02:19.082
Press Feedback in the top bar, write
a short note, and send it to the team.

00:02:19.847 --> 00:02:20.877
That is the whole tour.

00:02:21.087 --> 00:02:25.377
Models draft the pages, you correct them,
and every correction helps the next model.

